- in_text_citation: [(Li et al., 2024-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7c6eb48689139d94fc0f84882d611ed5b18e640e)
- venue: Communications Biology
- citation count: 8
[method]
The paper analyzed human intracranial EEG recordings from participants with electrodes localized to hippocampal subfields, including CA1, DG/CA3, and subiculum. After preprocessing and NREM sleep selection, the authors performed spectral analysis and time-frequency analysis to characterize delta, spindle, and ripple oscillations. They detected delta, spindle, and ripple events, then computed band-based cross-frequency coupling and event-based cross-frequency coupling, including ripple activity as a function of delta-spindle coupling phase and correlations between spindle power, ripple power, and delta slope. This is related to the proposed method because the proposed study also uses CA1 delta and ripple oscillations to identify slow-wave sleep and to examine ripple-associated hippocampal activity, although the paper does not use ACC, dual-site tetrodes, contextual fear conditioning, spike sorting, GLM decoding, or optogenetics.
[results]
The reported outcome was that delta-spindle-ripple coupling was observed in CA1 and subiculum but not in DG/CA3, and that hippocampal spindle and ripple events differentially regulated delta slope. The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical effect sizes, sample sizes, or p-values, so no quantitative values can be reported.
[context]
The experimental context was human intracranial EEG with electrode localization to hippocampal subfields, NREM sleep epoch selection, and interictal epileptic discharge detection. The analyses relied on spectral power, time-frequency representations, event detection, and cross-frequency coupling metrics for delta, spindle, and ripple activity. This differs from the proposed rodent dual-site ACC-CA1 tetrode recordings during contextual fear conditioning, which plan 8 tetrodes per region, manual spike sorting, peri-ripple histograms, GLM decoding, sublayer classification, and optogenetic stimulation.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Raybuck, and Lattal, 2011-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fabc760228a12707771f72a3d992e18f3f8bc812)
- venue: PLoS ONE
- citation count: 109
[method]
The paper’s relevant method was a behavioral fear-conditioning approach using contextual, trace, and delay paradigms with inactivation of dorsal hippocampus or amygdala. In the contextual condition, animals were exposed to a chamber and received footshock, then tested for freezing; in trace and delay conditions, a conditioned stimulus was paired with footshock at different CS-US intervals. Inactivation was applied during training or before/after training to test whether hippocampal or amygdala activity was required. This relates to the proposed method because both use a footshock-based contextual fear conditioning paradigm in a chamber, but the paper tests inactivation effects rather than simultaneous ACC-CA1 electrophysiology and ripple decoding.
[results]
The available section headings report that dorsal hippocampal inactivation impaired contextual fear conditioning but not delay fear conditioning, and impaired both contextual and trace fear conditioning. Amygdala inactivation did not impair trace fear conditioning, and the reported amygdala inactivation effects in trace conditioning were independent of the training protocol. Pre- or post-training amygdala inactivation impaired contextual but not trace fear conditioning. No quantitative freezing scores, sample sizes, p-values, or effect sizes are present in the provided excerpt.
[context]
The context is limited to the provided section titles: the study compared contextual, trace, and delay fear conditioning, used dorsal hippocampal or amygdala inactivation, and included pre-training and post-training inactivation conditions. Specific experimental parameters such as chamber dimensions, shock intensity, shock duration, number of shocks, CS-US intervals, inactivation agent, electrode/injection coordinates, and statistical tests are not provided in the excerpt.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Su et al., 2023-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d0d553b3a2f7ffa212c9a13864a2f276d3dcdf94)
- venue: Scientific Reports
- citation count: 8
[method]
The paper used a fear conditioning and memory recall paradigm to induce hippocampal memory-related transcriptional changes, followed by hippocampal tissue collection, RNA extraction, sequencing, bioinformatic differential gene expression analysis, cell type composition analysis, and validation by RT-qPCR and immunofluorescence. This relates to the proposed contextual fear conditioning learning paradigm because both use fear conditioning to establish a memory, but the paper’s method focuses on downstream hippocampal gene expression rather than simultaneous ACC-CA1 electrophysiology, ripple decoding, or optogenetic stimulation.
[results]
The paper reports that memory recall further segregated hippocampal gene expression in AS mice from WT mice, that differential gene expression was observed in hippocampus of WT and AS mice with or without memory recall, that GO and KEGG functional annotation revealed different pathways in WT and AS mice, that shared and distinct memory recall-induced DEGs were identified, and that memory recall-induced DEGs were validated. The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical fold changes, p-values, sample sizes, or statistical significance values.
[context]
The experiments used WT and AS mice and hippocampal tissue, comparing conditions with and without memory recall. The molecular workflow included RNA sequencing, bioinformatic processing, cell type composition analysis, RT-qPCR, and immunofluorescence validation. The provided text does not specify the fear conditioning chamber, footshock intensity, shock duration, number of shocks, inter-shock intervals, sleep periods, or electrophysiology hardware; the proposed method specifies a square shock-grid chamber, 3 min exploration, three 0.75 mA 0.5 s footshocks at 2-min intervals, 7.5 min training, 2-3 h pre- and post-training sleep, and a 5 min contextual fear test.
[end related work 2]

