[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Cheng et al., 2024-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/219234ee7edcb66f963db2c9997da9e0a5e3f89d)
- venue: Nature Communications
- citation count: 6
[method]
The directly related method in the paper is a contextual fear conditioning behavioral assay, listed as the Fear conditioning task, in mice with cholinergic-specific histamine H1R manipulation, including ChAT-Cre;Hrh1 fl/fl mice and regional cholinergic H1R models. The assay was used to test whether H1R signaling in cholinergic neurons affects contextual fear memory. The supplied excerpt does not state the chamber type, acclimation duration, number, intensity, or duration of footshocks, inter-shock intervals, or test timing, so it cannot be confirmed whether the paper used the proposed square shock-grid chamber, 3-minute exploration, three 0.75 mA/0.5 s shocks at 2-minute intervals, or continuous pre-training sleep, training, post-training sleep, and 5-minute test recording. The paper also lists electrophysiology, fiber photometry, and optogenetic/chemogenetic intervention, which are conceptually related to the proposed dual-site ACC-CA1 electrophysiology and optogenetic stimulation plan, but the excerpt does not provide electrode configurations, recording sites, stimulation parameters, or analysis pipeline details.
[results]
The results headings indicate that decreased H1R expression in cholinergic neurons selectively enhanced contextual fear memory; that H1R in the medial septum but not other cholinergic brain regions was critical for contextual fear memory; that the dentate gyrus was the major downstream region of medial septum cholinergic neurons responsible for the enhancement; that ChAT-Cre;Hrh1 fl/fl mice showed functional acetylcholine deficiency to dentate gyrus neurons during retrieval but not consolidation; and that H1R in dentate gyrus-projecting medial septum cholinergic neurons was essential for retrieval of contextual fear memory. No quantitative freezing values, sample sizes, effect sizes, or statistical test results are available in the supplied excerpt.
[context]
The experimental context was a genetic and circuit-level study of histamine H1R in cholinergic neurons, with medial septum and dentate gyrus as key regions, and with E-SARE induction experiments, optogenetic/chemogenetic intervention, fiber photometry, electrophysiology, and statistical analysis listed in the methods. The excerpt does not provide shock parameters, recording hardware, sleep staging criteria, GLM decoding details, or optogenetic stimulation parameters, so the direct overlap with the proposed dual-site ACC-CA1 electrophysiology and GLM decoding plan is limited to the shared use of contextual fear conditioning as the behavioral learning paradigm.
[end related work 3]


[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Murphy et al., 2013-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/47f566e0e06447a9febb6d228d13495eb53d0d08)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 0
[method]
The paper used optogenetic silencing of excitatory dorsal CA1 hippocampal neurons and anterior cingulate cortex neurons to test their causal roles in contextual fear memory. Excitatory dorsal CA1 neurons were virally targeted to express the chloride channel eNpHR3.1, and rapid optical stimulation was applied to inhibit these neurons during memory recall. The anterior cingulate cortex was also optogenetically inhibited to assess its contribution to context fear memory. This is related to the proposed method because both approaches use optogenetic manipulation to test causal contributions of cortical and hippocampal circuits in contextual fear conditioning, but the paper tested necessity by inhibiting CA1 and ACC rather than testing feed-forward effects by stimulating ACC with ChR2 and recording CA1 sublayer and interneuron responses during sleep.
[results]
Rapid optogenetic inhibition of dorsal CA1 excitatory neurons reversibly abolished both short-term and long-term contextual fear memory, including memories up to 9 weeks old. When CA1 inhibition was extended significantly, contextual fear memory became hippocampal independent, indicating that long-term memory normally relies on the hippocampus but can shift to other structures. Optogenetic inhibition of the anterior cingulate cortex impaired long-term contextual fear memory but did not impair recent contextual fear memory. No statistical values were reported in the provided excerpt.
[context]
The experiments used contextual fear conditioning, a hippocampal-dependent paradigm, and real-time optogenetic manipulation during memory recall rather than during sleep or sharp-wave ripple epochs. The relevant regions were dorsal CA1 excitatory neurons and the anterior cingulate cortex, with long-term retention tested up to 9 weeks. The paper did not record CA1 sublayer-specific or interneuron-specific responses, did not deliver ACC stimulation, and did not use 4-pulse 25 Hz stimulation; it instead used optogenetic inhibition to assess whether CA1 and ACC are required for context fear memory retention.
[end related work 1]

