- venue: Scientific Reports
- citation count: 19
[method]
The paper used AAV-mediated expression of ChR2 in cortical astrocytes, optogenetic stimulation, and multiphoton imaging to examine astrocytic calcium dynamics and slow-wave activity in young APP/PS1 and NTG mice. It also used open field and contextual fear conditioning tests to assess sleep-dependent memory performance. This relates to the proposed method and research plan because it is a causal optogenetic manipulation during sleep that tests effects on memory, but it differs from the proposed approach by targeting astrocytes rather than ACC excitatory neurons, and it does not include simultaneous CA1 tetrode recordings, 4-pulse 25 Hz stimulation, or sublayer/interneuron latency analyses.
[results]
The supplied excerpt reports qualitatively that astrocytic calcium transients associated with slow oscillations were disrupted in young APP/PS1 mice, that optogenetic activation of cortical astrocytes restored slow waves, and that rhythmic optogenetic stimulation in astrocytes restored sleep-dependent memory performance in APP mice. No numerical effect sizes, sample sizes, or statistical significance values are provided in the available text.
[context]
The experimental context involved young APP/PS1 and NTG mice, cortical astrocyte-specific ChR2 expression, slow oscillations/slow waves, sleep-dependent memory, and contextual fear conditioning. The method is relevant to the proposed plan’s use of optogenetic stimulation during sleep to test causal effects on memory, but it is not directly comparable to the proposed ACC-to-CA1 optogenetic experiment because it lacks dual-site ACC and CA1 electrophysiology, CaMKII-ChR2 expression, 25 Hz 4-pulse stimulation, and analysis of CA1 sublayers or interneuron populations.
