[method]
The paper used a time-varying l0 penalized auto-regressive model to jointly detect spikes and estimate firing rates from multi-trial calcium fluorescence traces. The algorithm alternated between spike detection and firing-rate estimation in an expectation-maximization-type procedure, with firing rates estimated using Gaussian-boxcar smoothing across time and trials. In the applications, the method was used to estimate trial-aligned firing-rate functions for individual neurons and to compare activity across behavioral outcome groups or learning stages. This is related to the proposed task-active/task-inactive categorization because both require estimating firing rates in defined behavioral epochs and comparing them to a reference condition, but the paper did not compute a firing activity index ratio or formally assign task-active or task-inactive labels.
[results]
In simulations with bimodal inhomogeneous Poisson spike trains and calcium traces, the time-varying penalty improved both spike detection and firing-rate estimation compared with a fixed penalty. At the optimal penalty, the mean VP distance for spike detection was reduced by 17.3% and the l2 firing-rate estimation error was reduced by 76.3%. In the whisker discrimination calcium imaging data, a pyramidal tract neuron in the anterior lateral motor cortex showed estimated spikes and elevated firing immediately after the cue in correct lick-left trials, with little activity in correct lick-right and incorrect trials, indicating outcome-selective task-related activity. In the hippocampal fear-conditioning calcium imaging data, a CA1 excitatory neuron showed estimated spikes and firing that were more synchronous to foot shock and more frequent in the relearning stage than in the learning stage. Assuming all trials shared one firing-rate distribution produced a misleading peak firing time, whereas stage-specific stratified estimates revealed the shift in firing timing and frequency.
[context]
The simulations used 50 trials, 1000 time points, a 50 Hz sampling rate, 20 seconds per trial, a decay parameter of 0.96, noise level 0.15, a 200 ms Gaussian kernel bandwidth, and 100 generated datasets per setting; additional settings with noise level 0.3, 2000 time points, and decay parameter 0.98 also showed improved accuracy. The first calcium imaging dataset was from one mouse with 73 trials recorded at 15 Hz, preprocessed with neuropil correction and deltaF/F0 transformation, and grouped into 31 correct lick-left, 21 correct lick-right, and 21 incorrect trials, with 200 ms within-trial Gaussian smoothing. The second calcium imaging dataset was from one mouse with 141 hippocampal CA1 excitatory neurons recorded over several weeks, using 21 foot-shock sessions comprising 11 learning sessions and 10 relearning sessions, recorded at 15 Hz for 2 minutes per session and aligned to shock onset, with 400 ms within-trial Gaussian-boxcar smoothing and a 5-trial across-trial window. These conditions are relevant to the proposed research plan because they demonstrate how firing-rate estimation and stage- or outcome-specific comparisons can reveal task-related changes in hippocampal and motor cortical neurons, but the proposed plan uses direct electrophysiology rather than calcium imaging and adds a pre-training baseline ratio for task-active and task-inactive classification.
